 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy (2Q)
+
+The cache uses 2Q replacement so that one-use sheets from a long sequential screening cannot displace the sheets editors return to for comparison.
+
+- **Probation queue**: a sheet is admitted here the first time its key is rendered. Probation is FIFO.
+- **Protected queue**: holds sheets that have been shown to be reused. Protected is LRU.
+- **Eviction history**: a metadata-only list of recently evicted probation keys, holding no JPEG data and not counted toward the 12 GiB bound. A key requested while it is in the history is admitted to the protected queue after rendering.
+- **Eviction order**: when space is needed, evict from probation first while probation exceeds its share of the cache; otherwise evict the least recently used protected sheet.
+
+The probation share is a worker configuration value. All queues, the history, and their counters are private to the preview worker; the HTTP contract, render queue concurrency, and 12 GiB bound are the same under every policy.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long one-pass screenings with repeated comparison of a few clips. LRU lets a screening evict useful comparison sheets. Aged frequency counts keep stale favorites after a project switch and add counter upkeep. 2Q protects reused sheets from scans at the cost of two queues and a share parameter. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Probation share of the cache, and the bound on the eviction history length.
+2. What counts as reuse: whether a cache hit on a probation sheet promotes it, or only a request that finds its key in the eviction history.
